  "brief_description": "Philips Wouwerman  has captured the violence and cruelty of battle as it was in seventeenth-century Europe, when Holland was closely involved in the wars that raged across the continent. Most of the scene is in shadow, with details picked out in one or two flashes of colour: a horse’s rump, a red jacket, a helmet, a fallen battle flag. The cold, brilliant light is more like cannon fire than sunlight.\n\nThis picture is one of the largest battle paintings that Wouwerman ever made and is one of his few dated works. The scene and its buildings, flags and uniforms are entirely imaginary. The realism comes from the dramatic composition and eerie lighting, the depiction of bodies and faces, and, perhaps above all, the portrayal of the horses, for which he was famous.",
